---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { relFor } from '../../lib/affiliate-value';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CategoryHub from '../../components/CategoryHub.astro';
import AffiliateDisclosure from '../../components/AffiliateDisclosure.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Donor CRM software for nonprofits"
  description="Donor CRMs compared by real price and database size, plus the interactive-spreadsheet option (monday.com, Jotform) that carries a lot of small shops before they buy one."
  section="donor-crm">
  <div class="wrap">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Donor CRM' }]} />
    <section class="prose" style="margin-top: 1.2rem;">
      <p class="kicker">Category guide</p>
      <h1>Donor CRM software</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The donor CRM is your database of record: every donor, gift, and
        relationship in one place. Credible options start at $39-$45 a month and
        most price by the size of your database, not your team. Plenty of small
        shops run on an interactive spreadsheet first, and that is a legitimate
        starting point.
      </p>

      <AffiliateDisclosure />
    </section>

    <CategoryHub category="donor-crm" part="table" />

    <section class="prose" style="max-width: none;">


      <h2>How to choose</h2>
      <p>
        Pick by who will run it, then price it over three years rather than one.
        Our default from your first real database through about $5M is
        <a href="/go/donordock" rel={relFor('donordock')}>DonorDock</a>
        (from ~$98/mo, 90-day guarantee): every tier includes unlimited contacts,
        so a growing file never re-prices your contract, and the ActionBoard
        drives the follow-up small teams keep dropping. On the strictest budget,
        <a href="/go/eleo" rel={relFor('eleo')}>Eleo</a> ($39/mo) and
        <a href="/go/littlegreenlight" rel={relFor('littlegreenlight')}>Little Green Light</a>
        ($45/mo) both publish their full price ladders, and both bill by record
        count. Past $5M, or anywhere retention reporting is the thing your team
        asks for most, <a href="/go/bloomerang" rel={relFor('bloomerang')}>Bloomerang</a>
        (from $79/mo) earns the higher bill. Membership and event-heavy
        organizations should demo <a href="/go/neoncrm" rel={relFor('neoncrm')}>Neon CRM</a>
        before buying separate tools.
      </p>
      <h2>The interactive-spreadsheet option</h2>
      <p>
        A lot of small shops run their donor list on
        <a href="/go/monday" rel={relFor('monday')}>monday.com</a> or
        <a href="/go/jotform" rel={relFor('jotform')}>Jotform</a>
        instead of buying a CRM, and for a few hundred donors it works. What you
        get is a shared, structured spreadsheet: custom fields for anything you
        want to track, filtered views per staff member, intake forms that write
        straight into the list, reminders on the next touch, and a bill of $0 to
        about $17 a month rather than $45 to $125. For a shop where the real
        problem is "our donor list lives in three places and one of them is a
        departed board member's inbox," that is a genuine upgrade.
      </p>
      <p>
        Be clear about what you are giving up, because it is the fundraising
        layer rather than the data layer. There is no gift receipting, no soft
        credits or household records, no lapsed-donor or retention reporting,
        and no giving history that a future CRM can import cleanly. You will
        build every report by hand.
      </p>
      <p>
        Move to a real CRM when any of these becomes true: you cannot answer
        "who gave last year but not this year" in under a minute; you are
        issuing receipts or year-end statements manually; gift records and
        contact records have started disagreeing; or a second person needs to
        touch the data and you find yourself explaining the conventions. Until
        then, an interactive spreadsheet you actually maintain beats a CRM you
        bought and never populated. If you want the middle path,
        <a href="/go/donordock" rel={relFor('donordock')}>DonorDock's</a>
        90-day money-back guarantee makes the first real CRM a low-risk test.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two habits save the most grief. Clean your list before you migrate,
        since most CRMs bill by record count. And run a test import during any
        trial to confirm gift history and soft credits arrive intact; a demo
        with your own data beats a demo with sample data.
      </p>
    </section>

    <CategoryHub category="donor-crm" part="tools" />

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The first real CRM enters at <a href="/stacks/250k-1m/">$250k-$1M</a>;
        see the upgrades at <a href="/stacks/1m-5m/">$1M-$5M</a> and
        <a href="/stacks/5m-plus/">$5M+</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
